{"id":4429,"date":"2021-12-06T15:25:33","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:25:33","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4429"},"modified":"2024-11-27T15:12:20","modified_gmt":"2024-11-27T23:12:20","slug":"1-6-conditionals-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-6-conditionals-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"1.6: Conditionals"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0deb3d\" name=\"6a2213a0deb3d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0dec93\" name=\"6a2213a0dec93\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain what a conditional is and how it is used to change a program&#8217;s flow<\/li>\n<li>Explain the order a conditional uses to evaluate its statements, as well as the order&#8217;s importance<\/li>\n<li>Describe how Python uses indentation to identify parts of a program<\/li>\n<li>Create an if \/ else conditional<\/li>\n<li>Identify the parts of a conditional<\/li>\n<li>Create a conditional with an elif (else if) condition<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What are If Statements?<\/li>\n<li>Use the Tab Key: The Importance of Indentation<\/li>\n<li>Creating a Condition<\/li>\n<li>Advanced Conditionals: The elif<\/li>\n<li>Who Needs Else?<\/li>\n<li>The If Order Ordeal<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Red Light, Green Light<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Different Altitudes, Different Attitudes<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0ded57\" name=\"6a2213a0ded57\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are If Statements?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>If statements<\/strong> are <strong>conditionals<\/strong>, which are used to help your program make decisions. You actually use conditionals every day! Some examples might include:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If my alarm goes off, then I will wake up. Else, I will stay asleep.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If I am hungry, I will eat something.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If it&#8217;s sunny out, I will wear sunscreen.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If my room is clean, I will get my allowance. Else, I will make no money.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Watch the video below to learn more about conditionals:<\/span><\/p>\n<p><iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"Intro to Conditionals with the CoDrone EDU\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" src=\"https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/embed\/2R0MJayXjrU?feature=oembed&#038;enablejsapi=1&#038;origin=https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen><\/iframe><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Some important rules to remember:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>\u201cIf\u201d tests a condition once. If the condition is true, it will do the steps indented under the \u201cif\u201d. Otherwise, it goes on to the next \u201cif\u201d or \u201celse\u201d.<\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201c<strong>Else<\/strong>\u201d has no condition. This is because \u201celse\u201d is the last option when using if-else statements.\u00a0 When a computer reaches the \u201celse\u201d option after checking all the other conditions, the else condition is automatically true and will run its statements.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One way to use \u201celse\u201d would be when something needs to happen when a condition is false. When the if condition is false and nothing needs to happen, you don\u2019t need to use an \u201celse\u201d.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0dede7\" name=\"6a2213a0dede7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Use the Tab Key: The Importance of Indentation<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Indentation<\/strong> is very important in Python. The indentations indicate which statements belong to a particular section in the code.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You may have noticed that the statements below the if and else statements are indented <\/span>4 spaces<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> or one <\/span>tab<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. If you want to have multiple statements executed if a condition is true using proper indentation, it would look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">battery_percentage = 50\r\n\r\nif battery_percentage &gt; 60:\r\n    print(\"Ready for takeoff!\")\r\n\r\nelse:\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0print(\"Battery too low.\")<\/pre>\n<p><em>Below is an incorrect example.<\/em> Try running it to see what happens.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drones = 100\r\n\r\nbugs = 55\r\n\r\nif drones &gt; bugs print(\"Squish the bugs!\")\r\n\r\nelse print(\"Fly away!\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The example should look something like this. Try running it now and change the values of drones and bugs.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drones = 100\r\n\r\nbugs = 55\r\n\r\nif drones &gt; bugs:\r\n\r\n    print(\"Squish the bugs!\")\r\n\r\nelse:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Fly away!\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0dee50\" name=\"6a2213a0dee50\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating a Condition<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create 3 variables with different numerical values. Next, write at least 3 if statements and an else statement. Use the variables as the conditions for the if statements. The statements of the if statements and else statement should print different strings. The program below is an example &#8212; try writing your own!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n \r\nx = 58\r\ny = 12\r\nz = 90\r\n \r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\nif x &gt; y:\r\n   drone.set_pitch(50)\r\n   drone.move(2)\r\nif z &gt; x:\r\n   drone.set_roll(50)\r\n   drone.move(2)\r\nif y+z &gt; x:\r\n   drone.set_yaw(50)\r\n   drone.move(2)\r\nif y &gt; z:\r\n   drone.set_throttle(50)\r\n   drone.move(2)\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0deec1\" name=\"6a2213a0deec1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Advanced Conditionals: The elif<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]We have seen that standard conditionals with an \u201cif\u201d and \u201celse\u201d will give us 2 possible outcomes. <em>But what if we want to have 3 or more possible outcomes?<\/em> Watch the video below to learn more about how we can make this happen:<\/p>\n<p>https:\/\/youtu.be\/auAb-yZ8KdM[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<em>So what are the rules with elif (pronounced else if)?<\/em><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The first \u201cif\u201d is evaluated. If it is true, it does this and skips the rest.<\/li>\n<li>If the first \u201cif\u201d is false, the \u201celif\u201d is evaluated. If true, it does the steps for the \u201celif\u201d and skips the rest.<\/li>\n<li>If both the \u201cif\u201d and \u201celif\u201d are false, the \u201celse\u201d steps are executed.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0def1a\" name=\"6a2213a0def1a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Who Needs Else?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that we know the primary parts of a conditional, <em>what happens when we don&#8217;t have all those parts?<\/em> <em>Can a conditional not have an else or not have an elif?<\/em> Watch the video below to find out!<\/p>\n<p>https:\/\/youtu.be\/14YEX8ApIrA[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now that we know we don&#8217;t need an elif or else, let&#8217;s think of some examples where we might want just an if statement. Come up with 5 examples where you might need only an if.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0def9b\" name=\"6a2213a0def9b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>The If Order Ordeal<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<em>Does the order of statements in a conditional matter?<\/em> Copy the code below and then watch the video to follow along!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">battery_power = 50\r\nif battery_power &lt; 40:\r\n    print(\"Battery is low.\")\r\nelif battery_power &lt; 20:\r\n    print(\"Battery level critical.\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"Battery normal.\")<\/pre>\n<p>https:\/\/youtu.be\/1h8dIqy19Uk[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now that we know order is important, try to find the error in the following code:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height = 35\r\nif height &gt; 20:\r\n     print(\"Ready for movements!\")\r\nif height &gt; 40:\r\n     print(\"Altitude limit exceeded!\")\r\nelif height &gt; 30: \r\n     print (\"Warning! Approaching altitude limit\")<\/pre>\n<p>Additionally, answer the following questions about the code above:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>How many conditionals are there?<\/li>\n<li>What happens if your height variable is 35?<\/li>\n<li>What happens if your height variable is 15?<\/li>\n<li>What happens if your height variable is 25?<\/li>\n<li>What would you do to fix the error in this code?<\/li>\n<li>Will the program still run with the code as it is shown above?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Now, create and run your own code that has an ordering error in the conditional. Demonstrate how different values can return unexpected or unwanted results.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0df020\" name=\"6a2213a0df020\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Red Light, Green Light<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-6359 size-medium\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/03\/9_traffic-light-300x265.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"265\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/03\/9_traffic-light-300x265.png 300w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/03\/9_traffic-light-1024x904.png 1024w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/03\/9_traffic-light.png 1513w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" \/><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>In this challenge, we will be playing a version of the \u201cRed Light, Green Light\u201d game with our drone to practice working with conditionals! For this challenge you will need to pick a color between red, yellow, and green. Create a variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">light_color<\/code> and assign the color you chose.<\/p>\n<p>If the light is green, we want to take off and fly forward at a normal speed.<br \/>\nIf the light is yellow, then we want to take off and fly forward at a slow speed.<br \/>\nIf the light is red, we want to take off, hover, and then land.<\/p>\n<p>If you are completing this challenge on your own, make sure that all three colors are working as you intend them too! Here is one possible solution to the challenge:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nlight_color = \"green\"\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nif light_color == \"green\":\r\n    drone.set_pitch(50)\r\n    drone.move(2)\r\n\r\nelif light_color == \"yellow\":\r\n    drone.set_pitch(30)\r\n    drone.move(1)\r\n\r\nelif light_color == \"red\":\r\n    drone.hover(3)\r\n\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"light_color needs to be red, yellow, or green!\")\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>The else statement in this example uses something known as error handling. If you were to accidentally incorrectly spell your color, or if you use a color that isn&#8217;t red, yellow, or green, the program will print out a message asking you to check your color and try again![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<a id=\"6a2213a0df0b9\" name=\"6a2213a0df0b9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Conditions and Flight Movements<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This challenge will be checking a variable and making a flight movement based on the string that variable holds.<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You\u2019ll declare a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">flight_movement<\/code>,<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> which will be a string saying forward, backward, left, right, up, etc.<\/span><\/li>\n<li>You can code as many movements as you would like, but the program must have forward, backward, left, and right.<\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Check the direction variable and have your drone do the following:<\/span><\/li>\n<li>If the direction is forward, have your drone fly forward for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<li>If the direction is backward, have your drone fly backward for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<li>If the direction is right, have your drone fly to the right for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<li>If the direction is left, have your drone fly to the left for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<li>If you have any unique flight movements you would like to add, feel free! You can even bring your old sine wave, square, or circle code into this program.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a0df119\" name=\"6a2213a0df119\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about conditions in programming! You learned how conditionals can be used to change a program&#8217;s outcome, as well as how to use them in an if\/else statements.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 12 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-6-conditionals-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4734,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,4328,14401,2119,2123,2122,2134],"class_list":["post-4429","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-conditionals","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4429","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":30,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4429\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13315,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4429\/revisions\/13315"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4734"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4429"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4429"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4429"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}